VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W2
ATTEMPT: 3
---
Baseline (not disputed): oracle .swarm/tier3/W2/accept.sh reproduces
"ORACLE PASS" (8/8 checks) when re-run from a fresh clone of the working
tree; `go build ./...`, `go vet ./...` exit 0; package test suite green.
No css-verify script exists in this tree (nothing to check there).

Reachable defect the oracle does not catch — locale-dependent JS overwrite
fires on EVERY page load, not only on drag:

1. Trace: `document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...)` in
   quick-adjust-scripts.html:223-226 calls `syncAllQuickAdjustControls()`
   unconditionally. That iterates every non-mirror `[data-quick-adjust-key]`
   control and calls `syncQuickAdjustKey(key)`. For
   key === 'monthly_living_expenses' this calls both
   `syncQuickAdjustDisplays(key, canonicalControl.value)` (→
   `formatQuickAdjustDisplay` → `formatWholeDollars`, quick-adjust-scripts.html:41)
   AND `updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote(canonicalControl.value)`
   (portfolio-settings.html:163, new in this attempt — see git diff below).
   So on every load (and again after every htmx:afterSwap/oobAfterSwap via
   scheduleQuickAdjustSync), the server-rendered `$7,386` display span, its
   aria-valuetext, the quick-adjust mirror display, and the phase note's
   "Engine spends $X.XX/mo now" figure are all immediately recomputed and
   overwritten by JS — not merely on drag.

2. Both recomputations route through `Number.prototype.toLocaleString()`
   called with no explicit locale (`Math.round(v).toLocaleString()` in
   formatWholeDollars, and `(base*multiplier).toLocaleString(undefined,
   {minimumFractionDigits:2,maximumFractionDigits:2})` in the brand-new
   updateLivingExpensesPhaseNote). Per spec, `toLocaleString()` without a
   locale argument uses the runtime's default locale (browser
   language/OS setting in production; ICU env in Node). Reproduced directly:

     $ node -e 'console.log((7387).toLocaleString())'
     7,387
     $ LANG=de_DE.UTF-8 node -e 'console.log((7387).toLocaleString())'
     7.387

   For any user whose browser locale is not an en-US-style locale (a very
   ordinary configuration — most of continental Europe, for one), the exact
   same numeric value renders as a DIFFERENT string ("$7.387" vs the
   server's canonical "$7,386") the instant the page loads or any OOB swap
   settles. Locales with non-Latin digit systems (e.g. ar-EG) would replace
   the digits entirely, not just the separator.

3. This is precisely the bug class Tier 3 was escalated to kill ("ONE
   dollar string per value, everywhere" — accept.sh header comment) and
   that the worker's own code comment claims to guarantee: "SINGLE JS
   whole-dollar formatter: round half-away-from-zero, thousands
   separators, leading $" (quick-adjust-scripts.html:7-10). That guarantee
   is false outside en-US-style locales. It is not a hypothetical: it is
   demonstrated above, and the DOMContentLoaded trace shows it fires
   unconditionally, not only under a drag interaction the oracle can't
   reach.

4. The accept.sh oracle does not catch this because it only (a) renders Go
   templates directly (never executes the JS) and (b) does structural
   greps for formatter-count, not locale-safety; accept.sh's own comment on
   check 04a explicitly carves the `toLocaleString(undefined, {...})` cents
   call out as "a different, allowed format" — a carve-out about *shape*
   (2-decimal vs whole-dollar), not about locale independence, and it is
   this exact carved-out call site that is newly introduced in this attempt
   and newly locale-unsafe:

     $ git diff master -- web/templates/components/whatif/portfolio-settings.html | grep -n toLocaleString
     +    amount.textContent = '$' + (base * multiplier).toLocaleString(undefined, {

   (The old code this replaced used unqualified `.toLocaleString()` too, so
   the locale-hazard is not newly created in the abstract, but this
   specific call site is new, was written for this task, and was not fixed
   as part of "the one-string contract" the task claims to now enforce.)

Secondary, lower-confidence note (not required to reach FAIL, offered for
completeness): `formatWholeDollars` JS negates asymmetrically vs the Go
`formatWholeDollars`/`formatDollars` for negative inputs — Go emits
"-$500", JS's `'$' + Math.round(-500).toLocaleString()` emits "$-500" (a
different string, not just a different locale). The slider/UI cannot
currently drive MonthlyLivingExpenses negative (range min="0" on both the
canonical hidden field's paired range and phase-multiplier min="0.30"), so
I could not confirm this path is reachable through the UI alone; it would
require a hand-edited settings file. Not load-bearing for this verdict.

Ruling: the locale-drift finding (points 1-4) is a reachable, reproduced,
same-value/two-different-strings case surviving in this attempt, on a code
path (DOMContentLoaded → syncAllQuickAdjustControls) that runs unconditionally
for every user, not an edge-case timing race. Per the dispatch instructions
("PASS only if no reachable same-value-two-strings case remains") this is a
FAIL.
